mppgo
=====

This library reads Microsoft Project files in Go.

The MPP file format is proprietary and undocumented. Microsoft has never
published a specification for it. Everything this library knows about the
binary layout of an MPP file — the meaning of the FixedMeta/FixedData and
VarMeta/Var2Data block structures, the Props key values, the calendar data
encoding, the date/time epoch, and the stream obfuscation scheme — derives
from the reverse engineering work of the MPXJ project:

    MPXJ - https://github.com/joniles/mpxj
    Copyright (c) Packwood Software
    Licensed under the GNU Lesser General Public License v2.1 or later

MPXJ's Java source was read as the reference for understanding the format,
and parts of this library follow it closely:

  - mpp/fielddefs_gen.go is generated from MPXJ's MPP field tables
    (MPPTaskField, MPPResourceField, MPPAssignmentField and the data types
    in TaskField, ResourceField and AssignmentField): field indexes, names
    and data types.
  - The decoding of actual (complete) timephased work, including the
    splicing of irregular work ranges (mpp/timephased.go), is a Go
    translation of MPXJ's TimephasedDataFactory.getCompleteWork and
    splitItem.
  - Field-map decoding, lookup-table values, recurring calendar exceptions,
    work weeks, recurring tasks, hyperlinks, bit-flag positions and the
    project property keys follow the corresponding MPXJ readers.

The rest of the Go implementation was written independently against that
understanding. Either way, the format knowledge that makes this library
possible is MPXJ's contribution, and that debt is acknowledged here
explicitly. MPXJ is licensed under the LGPL; whether the parts above are
derived works for licensing purposes has not been assessed here.

If you need broad, production-grade Microsoft Project support today —
including MPP8/9/12, Primavera, Asta, Planner and many other formats — use
MPXJ. It is mature, actively maintained, and available for Java, .NET,
Python and Ruby.
